    "cards": [
        {
            "dot": "cyan",
            "title": "Three agents, not one",
            "items": [
                "The agent in VS Code is the third: it writes the tour, restarts the backend, calls the routes and reads the pictures",
                "The other two are copilot runtimes on separate LAN hosts, each with its own token, its own model name and its own workspace",
                "One account per host is the rule, so a run names which machine worked it and a second run can use the other",
                "The same task file is the prompt for every run, which is what makes two runs comparable"
            ]
        },
        {
            "dot": "emerald",
            "title": "The script is a harness you can watch",
            "items": [
                "runTask.sh claims the first free ticket and prints the account, the ticket, the session id and where to look",
                "It checks the account's kind and mode before claiming, so a runtime that is down is told apart from a token that is wrong",
                "It leaves the run in the foreground: Ctrl-C parks the session instead of losing it",
                "The task it runs sleeps between steps on purpose, so a person has two minutes to watch rather than a transcript that arrives whole"
            ]
        },
        {
            "dot": "violet",
            "title": "What proves it ran",
            "items": [
                "The partition on the session row is a broker delivery report: a run through the in-memory ring has none",
                "The page replays the same session after a reload, which is the topic retaining it rather than a client remembering it",
                "It is served by its own Vite dev server on 5173, so what the screenshots show is a real origin rather than a fixture",
                "The transcript's cards are the payloads read once: a command and its exit code, a diff, a question and the answer a person gave",
                "The browser spec drives its own ports and compares both palettes, so a rendering defect fails a run instead of a review"
            ]
        }
    ]
